  "explanation": {
    "id": "68f9b415-c372-4058-97c1-122d3b4e17f0",
    "body": "A common mistake that one might make on this question is to say that the radius of convergence would be $2\\leq R<3$. The reason this answer is incorrect is because it is too restrictive of an answer. The rules of a power series state that convergence or divergence at a boundary is uncertain unless specified otherwise. Meaning that the radius of convergence needs to include the boundary case just in case the series does converge regardless of the fact that the problem states that the series diverges at x = 4. In other words, a power series converges when $|x-a| < R$, diverges when $|x-a| > R$, and can either converge or diverge when $|x-a| = R$. \n\nThis is similar to including the boundary in an interval of convergence when dealing with a series converges conditionally at the boundary.",
    "questionId": "da64a574-a050-486a-a121-d4540ef6d32f",
